## Startup Synchronization

Every repository task starts by reading `AGENTS.md` and checking Git consistency as one required step. The startup check covers worktree status, remotes, `git fetch backup --prune`, and the relationship between `HEAD`, local `main`, and `backup/main`.

Implementation, commit, push, and PR creation do not proceed until that startup synchronization is complete.

The local Git consistency baseline is `fetch.prune=true`, `pull.ff=only`, `remote.pushDefault=backup`, `push.default=current`, and `push.autoSetupRemote=true`. Local `main` tracks `backup/main`. `origin` remains available as a same-repository fallback remote, while `backup` is the default push target.

GitHub requires changes to `main` to pass through a pull request. Repository merge policy allows merge commits only, disables squash and rebase merging, keeps automatic head-branch deletion enabled, and allows an out-of-date PR branch to be updated. `main` protection blocks force pushes and branch deletion; it requires no approving review and no status check until those controls are separately adopted.

After a PR is merged by the user, the next repository task treats post-merge cleanup as part of startup synchronization. The cleanup verifies the merged PR state, fetches `backup --prune`, fast-forwards local `main` to `backup/main`, and deletes only the matching merged local branch whose head is already contained in `backup/main`.

Routine post-merge cleanup is scoped to the matching merged branch for the PR just handled. Older unrelated branches are never deleted automatically. The release check reports every local branch and `backup/*` remote-tracking branch already contained in `backup/main` as a stale merged branch; resolving any additional reported branch requires an explicit cleanup scope.

## Technical Baseline

- TypeScript assumes the Deno runtime.
- Standard adoption libraries are limited to Deno standard libraries when needed.
- npm compatibility packages, npm dependencies, Node.js dependencies, external frontend frameworks, external frontend build configuration files, CSS preprocessors, CSS minification, CSS bundling, `dist/`, `build/`, and `node_modules/` are prohibited.
- JSON is the only structured file format for development, build, generation, check, and release-check settings; YAML development/build configuration files are prohibited.
- CSS and JavaScript are managed as separate outputs.
- Generated outputs are kept in the repository and checked against local rules.

## Development Configuration And Output Placement

The Development configuration contract forbids YAML as a technology selection for development, build, generation, check, and release-check configuration. JSON is the only file-backed structured configuration format for those scopes.

The same contract forbids npm, Node, npm-compatible package managers, and external frontend build tool configuration. `package.json`, lockfiles for npm-compatible package managers, `node_modules/`, npm registry configuration, webpack, Vite, Rollup, Parcel, PostCSS, Tailwind, Babel, Gulp, Grunt, tsup, and esbuild configuration files must not be introduced.

The Generated output placement contract allows CSS and JavaScript files only at the explicit generated and sample-support paths listed in this specification. Generated CSS paths are `Tokens/*.css`, `UI/*.css`, and `EditorUI/wysiwyg.css`. Generated JavaScript paths are `UI/components.js`, `UI/forms.js`, `UI/content.js`, `EditorUI/wysiwyg.js`, and `EditorUI/editor.js`. Sample-support CSS and JavaScript paths are limited to `Samples/design/sample.css` and `Samples/design/sample.js`.

Generated output names must not introduce `dist/`, `build/`, `Dist/`, `Build/`, minified CSS/JavaScript files, bundled CSS/JavaScript files, or alternate generated output directories.

The TypeScript source inventory contract keeps every TypeScript source file check-covered. CSS generator sources must be listed by the CSS compiler registry, UI behavior sources must be listed by the JavaScript pair and interaction contracts, and structured editor sources must be listed by the editor runtime module registry.

The Source/output/sample boundary contract keeps TypeScript sources, generated outputs, and sample-support files separate. `TypeScript/` owns source, `Tokens/`, `UI/`, and `EditorUI/` own generated outputs, and `Samples/` owns non-authoritative confirmation material only.

